    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of X",
    "problem": "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $y=4{x}^{2}-1$ and $y=3{x}^{2}.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/4e3758ddf652c3dd7df10d381b42ca1dde50d48250bed672b740329191202597.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/54ad3b4c8bc3aef707af764cd8d8badf9390737dc75491aa66ea554f531a7576.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4{x}^{2}-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=3{x}^{2}.$\n\nWe can now set up the integration with our $x$ values (Top-Bottom):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(3{x}^{2})-(4{x}^{2}-1)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the integrand is an even function. Therefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2((1-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3})-0) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
